{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-stepwise-analysis", "file": "references/action-discipline-after-ambiguous-demos.md", "content": "# Action discipline after ambiguous demonstrations

Repeatedly requesting examples without recording the newly returned evidence can exhaust the budget while leaving the hypothesis unchanged. For every returned pair, immediately compare changed and preserved cells, update candidate rules, and identify concrete differing predictions on the test before deciding the next action. Unrelated-looking examples should be normalized by geometric and palette roles, but do not infer a shared rule unless it predicts every pair.

After the demo cap, stop requesting. Choose a rule only after constructing its exact test change set and checking it against all demos. If feedback rejects a submission, discard that full hypothesis and change the model in a material, evidence-based way; cosmetic changes to fill extent or endpoints are not a new hypothesis. Avoid repeated tool-based candidate construction without new information. Validate dimensions, widths, preserved features, then emit exactly one permitted JSON action immediately.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545235/attempts/492400b39f75449d8b5e6a1d637f2df9/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-demo-stepwise-analysis/references/action-discipline-after-ambiguous-demos.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-analysis", "file": "references/action-feedback-lessons.md", "content": "# Action-only ARC turns after submission feedback

When solving a costed interactive ARC instance, binary feedback makes each rejected grid valuable negative evidence. Keep a compact ledger of rejected candidate grids or their defining rules, and exclude them explicitly before developing the next candidate. Reconstruct from the complete demo set rather than tweaking a rejected output by intuition. A new candidate should be structurally distinct and validated against all input-output pairs, including preserved cells, dimensions, and row widths.

If no adequately supported candidate remains, use a demo request only when competing hypotheses are named and predict different outcomes that the next example can resolve. Do not spend another submission merely to probe; attempts also cost points. Keep each puzzle turn action-only: exactly one permitted JSON action on the final line, with no unrelated commands or explanatory response text.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545235/attempts/492400b39f75449d8b5e6a1d637f2df9/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-demo-analysis/references/action-feedback-lessons.md"}
